                <pb facs="0870" n="800" sameAs="frus:frus1883/pg800"/>

                <head>No. 510.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Cramer to Mr. Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Berne</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-11-16"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 16, 1882</date>.
                        (Received December 1.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 18.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that on the
                    13th instant a communication was received by this legation from Mr. Byers,
                    United States consul at Zurich, relating to the shipment to New York of an
                    improper person—a pauper and drunkard—by the town authorities of Woelflin-swyl,
                    canton of Aargau, in violation of the laws of the United States and of
                    Switzerland on the subject of emigration. A copy of Consul Byers’ letter is
                    herewith inclosed. As it was too late to send a cablegram, requesting the
                    Department to prohibit that person from landing, I felt it my duty to address a
                    note to the President of the Swiss Confederation, protesting against the
                    proceedings of the town authorities in the case of the said pauper, and
                    requesting the punishment of the guilty parties. A copy of this note is herewith
                    inclosed.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>M. J. CRAMER.</signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d509-a1.xml" xpointer="d509-a1"/>
                <xi:include href="d509-a2.xml" xpointer="d509-a2"/>
